zn-gate: disclosure for the npm dual-use content policy

Package:   zn-gate
Source:    https://github.com/usezn/zn
Security:  https://github.com/usezn/zn/blob/main/SECURITY.md

This package is a defensive security tool. Some of what it does can look like
malware to an automatic scanner, because protecting a computer from a coding
agent means touching the same places an attacker would: the configuration of
other programs, the permissions of files, and the way programs are started.
This file says what each of those things is for, and when it happens.

1. What zn-gate is

zn-gate is a local security layer for coding agents. It runs on the computer of
the person who installs it, between the agent and the files, commands and tools
the agent uses. It blocks the agent from reading files that hold secrets, hides
secret values in what tools return, checks text for prompt injection, reviews
MCP servers, and keeps secrets in an encrypted vault on the user's own computer.
It does not need an account. Its source code is open (MIT license).

2. Hooks in the configuration of other programs

zn-gate adds hooks to the configuration of the coding agents it protects:
Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, OpenCode, Antigravity, the ZCode desktop app
and, as experimental support, Gemini CLI and GitHub Copilot CLI. A hook is an
entry that tells the agent to ask zn-gate before it runs a tool, reads a file or
sends a prompt. This happens only when the person runs `zn hooks install` or
`zn-gate init` in their own terminal (`init` asks before each change). Nothing
is written when the package is installed or imported. `zn-gate uninstall` lists
what zn-gate added (hooks, wrapped MCP servers, the Claude Code plugin) and
removes it after asking. The hooks also guard their own files, so that the agent
cannot switch the protection off by editing them.

3. PowerShell and file permissions on Windows

On Windows, zn-gate starts the PowerShell that ships with the system, by its
full path inside the Windows folder, never one found through the PATH or the
project folder. It runs short inline scripts with -Command or -EncodedCommand,
for these things only:
  - to make the vault and zn's hook files readable only by the current user.
    This uses .NET access-control calls that replace the permission list of
    those folders and files with a single entry for that user; zn-gate then
    reads the list back and checks it;
  - to keep the vault key in the Windows Credential Manager, through a few lines
    of C# that PowerShell compiles in memory to call the system's own credential
    functions;
  - to show a confirmation window;
  - to open the browser at the sign-in page when the person runs
    `zn-gate connect`, and to read the clipboard when the person passes
    --clipboard to store a secret;
  - to run zn's own hook once when the person runs `zn-gate check`.
  - to give the vault's local named pipe the same single-user permission list;
  - to read, without changing them, the permission lists of a script and of
    every folder above it before the vault passes a secret to that script, and
    of the folder that holds zn's hook launcher and the folders above it, so
    that zn refuses a place that another account can change;
  - to read the list of running programs (name, parent and command line) when a
    command that only a person should run, such as `zn-gate init`,
    `zn-gate uninstall`, `zn vault purge` or adding a secret, checks whether a
    coding agent started it. The list is kept in memory only; it is not saved
    or sent.
The hooks that zn-gate writes for an agent on Windows also start this
PowerShell to run zn-gate. Nothing is downloaded for this, no user or group is
created or changed, and the permission changes apply only to zn-gate's own
folders, files and named pipe.

4. Deleting the vault

`zn vault purge` deletes the vault on the user's own computer (the encrypted
secrets, their token maps, the audit log, the backups and the session key) and
the vault key that zn-gate stored in the system keychain. It lists every file
it will delete, and the person has to type a confirmation phrase in their own
terminal. It refuses to run when an AI agent is the one running it (a script
with no terminal and no agent can pass --yes). It deletes only zn-gate's own
files and the keychain entry zn-gate created.

5. No install scripts, no downloaded code, no telemetry by default

The package has no preinstall, install or postinstall script. It does not
download or run code from the network; everything it runs is in the package. It
collects no telemetry unless the person turns it on. Network use is limited to
features the person chooses: the optional cloud check (`zn-gate connect`, which
talks to https://api.usezn.com), zn Team after the person joins an organization,
HTTP requests that the secrets MCP server makes for an agent, only to the hosts
the person allowed for each secret (`zn secret allow --host`), and, off by
default, pseudonymous daily counts that `zn-gate stats share on` sends after
printing them. Hooks, the vault, the shield and every other command work
locally. The local dashboard listens on 127.0.0.1 only.

